    <schema:name>4.4661</schema:name>
    <schema:position rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">265</schema:position>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/895">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/Thesis"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/Chapter"/>
    <ex:hasTextFragment rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/895-german"/>
    <ex:hasTextFragment rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/895-ogden"/>
    <ex:hasTextFragment rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/895-pears"/>
    <schema:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/893"/>
    <schema:mentions rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/topic/truth-operation-by-wittgenstein"/>
    <schema:name>5.522</schema:name>
    <schema:position rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">369</schema:position>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/1012-pears">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/TextFragment"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/CreativeWork"/>
    <schema:exampleOfWork rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/1012"/>
    <schema:inLanguage>en</schema:inLanguage>
    <schema:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/expression/pears"/>
    <schema:text xml:lang="en">Mechanics is an attempt to construct according to a single plan all the true propositions that we need for the description of the world.</schema:text>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/1019-pears">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/TextFragment"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/CreativeWork"/>
    <schema:exampleOfWork rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/1019"/>
    <schema:inLanguage>en</schema:inLanguage>
    <schema:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/expression/pears"/>
    <schema:text xml:lang="en">Kant’s problem about the right hand and the left hand, which cannot be made to coincide, exists even in two dimensions. Indeed, it exists in one-dimensional space</schema:text>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/880-pears">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/TextFragment"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/CreativeWork"/>
    <schema:exampleOfWork rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/880"/>
    <schema:inLanguage>en</schema:inLanguage>
    <schema:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/expression/pears"/>
    <schema:text xml:lang="en">All that is required is that we should construct a system of signs with a particular number of dimensions—with a particular mathematical multiplicity.</schema:text>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/540-ogden">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/TextFragment"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/CreativeWork"/>
    <schema:exampleOfWork rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/540"/>
    <schema:inLanguage>en</schema:inLanguage>
    <schema:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/expression/ogden"/>
    <schema:text xml:lang="en">If I know an object, then I also know all the possibilities of its occurrence in atomic facts. (Every such possibility must lie in the nature of the object.) A new possibility cannot subsequently be found.</schema:text>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/715-ogden">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/TextFragment"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/CreativeWork"/>
    <schema:exampleOfWork rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/715"/>
    <schema:inLanguage>en</schema:inLanguage>
    <schema:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/expression/ogden"/>
    <schema:text xml:lang="en">If we tried, for example, to express what is expressed by “(x). fx” by putting an index before fx, like: “  Gen.  fx”, it would not do, we should not know what was generalized. If we tried to show it by an index g, like: “f(x  g )” it would not do—we should not know the scope of the generalization. If we were to try it by introducing a mark in the argument places, like “(G, G). F(G, G)”, it would not do—we could not determine the identity of the variables, etc. All these ways of symbolizing are inadequate because they have not the necessary mathematical multiplicity.</schema:text>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/839-ogden">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/TextFragment"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/CreativeWork"/>
    <schema:exampleOfWork rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/839"/>
    <schema:inLanguage>en</schema:inLanguage>
    <schema:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/expression/ogden"/>
    <schema:text xml:lang="en">The sense of a truth-function of p is a function of the sense of p. Denial, logical addition, logical multiplication, etc., etc., are operations. (Denial reverses the sense of a proposition.)</schema:text>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/948-german">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/TextFragment"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/CreativeWork"/>
    <schema:exampleOfWork rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/948"/>
    <schema:inLanguage>de</schema:inLanguage>
    <schema:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/tractatus"/>
    <schema:text xml:lang="de">Dies sagt nichts anderes, als dass jeder Satz ein Resultat der successiven Anwendung der Operation  N’( ξ ) auf die Elementarsätze ist.</schema:text>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/1025">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/Thesis"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/Chapter"/>
    <ex:hasTextFragment rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/1025-german"/>
    <ex:hasTextFragment rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/1025-ogden"/>
    <ex:hasTextFragment rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/1025-pears"/>
