  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/886">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/Thesis"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/Chapter"/>
    <ex:hasTextFragment rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/886-german"/>
    <ex:hasTextFragment rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/886-ogden"/>
    <ex:hasTextFragment rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/886-pears"/>
    <schema:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/882"/>
    <schema:mentions rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/topic/truth-operation-by-wittgenstein"/>
    <schema:name>5.51</schema:name>
    <schema:position rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">360</schema:position>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/671-pears">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/TextFragment"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/CreativeWork"/>
    <schema:exampleOfWork rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/671"/>
    <schema:inLanguage>en</schema:inLanguage>
    <schema:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/expression/pears"/>
    <schema:text xml:lang="en">The reason why a function cannot be its own argument is that the sign for a function already contains the prototype of its argument, and it cannot contain itself. For let us suppose that the function F(fx) could be its own argument: in that case there would be a proposition ‘F(F(fx))’, in which the outer function F and the inner function F must have different meanings, since the inner one has the form ϕ(fx) and the outer one has the form ψ(ϕ(fx)). Only the letter ‘F’ is common to the two functions, but the letter by itself signifies nothing. This immediately becomes clear if instead of ‘F(F u)’ we write ‘(∃ ϕ): F(ϕu). ϕu = Fu’. That disposes of Russell’s paradox.</schema:text>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/fragment/1021-german">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/TextFragment"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://schema.org/CreativeWork"/>
    <schema:exampleOfWork rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/1021"/>
    <schema:inLanguage>de</schema:inLanguage>
    <schema:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/tractatus"/>
    <schema:text xml:lang="de">Der Vorgang der Induktion besteht darin, dass wir das einfachste Gesetz annehmen, das mit unseren Erfahrungen in Einklang zu bringen ist.</schema:text>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/unit/632">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/wittgensteiniana/Thesis"/>
